The recent commit <code>[vm] Fix extract_abort_info for compiler-generated abort codes (#19090)</code> made significant changes to the <code>AptosVM</code> implementation in <code>aptos-vm/src/aptos_vm.rs</code>. Specifically, the <code>extract_abort_info</code> method was modified to handle compiler-generated abort codes. The change introduces a conditional check for the <code>VM_BINARY_FORMAT_V10</code> feature flag, and if enabled, it calls the new <code>extract_abort_info</code> method; otherwise, it falls back to the legacy <code>extract_abort_info_legacy</code> method. <pre><code>if self.features().is_enabled(FeatureFlag::VM_BINARY_FORMAT_V10) {
    m.extract_abort_info(code)
} else {
    m.extract_abort_info_legacy(code)
}</code></pre> The new <code>extract_abort_info</code> method in <code>types/src/vm/module_metadata.rs</code> checks the error map for the given abort code and returns the corresponding <code>AbortInfo</code> if found. If not, it attempts to extract the reason from the lower 2 bytes of the code if the upper 5 bytes are zero. <pre><code>self.error_map.get(&code).or_else(|| {
    // Only extract the reason for canonical std::error codes (upper 5 bytes zero).
    if code >> 24 == 0 {
        let reason = code & 0xFFFF;
        self.error_map.get(&reason)
    } else {
        None
    }
})</code></pre> This change is part of the Move VM subsystem and affects the execution pipeline, where the <code>AptosVM</code> is responsible for executing Move bytecode. The implications of this change are that it correctly handles compiler-generated abort codes and provides more accurate error information. The <code>error_map_unspecified_abort_code</code> test in <code>e2e-move-tests/src/tests/error_map.rs</code> ensures that the <code>extract_abort_info</code> method does not return abort info for unspecified abort codes.
